ie.manual.entry.lithography.name=Lithography Machine
ie.manual.entry.lithography.subtext=Prints the mask pattern onto wafers
ie.manual.entry.lithography0=Build it as shown; mirroring is fine. A §llight source§r goes in the middle of the third layer. Then use an Engineer's Hammer on the §lAlignment Table§r.
ie.manual.entry.lithography1=The §lAlignment Table§r is the heart of the machine: microscope, mask holder and interface all sit in this block.<br>It stands inside the yellow glass booth, at eye level.<br>The recipe takes an Engineer's Workbench or an Industrial Workbench.
ie.manual.entry.lithography2=The §llight source§r sits right above the Alignment Table and shines down.<br>A Mercury Lamp and a Floodlight make a §lMercury Light Source§r. The lamp is built at the <link;workbench;§o§nEngineer's §o§nWorkbench§r> with the Light Source blueprint.<br>Craft the light source on its own to get the lamp back.
ie.manual.entry.lithography3=§lPipes at the back§r<br>All five fluid ports are at the back and take pipes from behind.<br><br>§lBlast brick end§r<br>Bottom: steam, distilled water out<br>Top: steam in<br><br>§lGlass end§r<br>Bottom, inner: developer in<br>Bottom, corner: etchant in<br>Top: photoresist in
ie.manual.entry.lithography4=§lPower§r<br>The Light Engineering Block in the middle of the back, upper row, takes power on any side.<br>§lItems§r<br>The hopper at the blast brick end takes items from outside. The dropper at the glass end pushes chips into a chest next to it.<br>§lRedstone§r<br>A signal on the Redstone Engineering Block at the front corner halts the machine. The hammer inverts it.
ie.manual.entry.lithography5=§lWhat to supply§r<br>§lFluids§r<br>Steam: IC2 steam, superheated steam<br>Photoresist<br>Developer: arenes, cresol, creosote, UU-Matter<br>Etchant: hydrochloric acid<br><br>§lItems§r<br>Precision Silicon Wafers<br>Dopant: boron ingot and glowstone dust, or boron and phosphorus<br>Aluminium dust<br>IC2 item casings, any metal
ie.manual.entry.lithography6=§lOpening the interface§r<br>Step into the yellow glass booth and rightclick the Alignment Table or the yellow glass.<br><br>§lLoading§r<br>The photomask can only be put in by hand; the hopper will not take it. The mask decides which chip is made.<br>Wafers, additives and casings go in by hand or through the hopper. Each slot takes only its own kind of item.
ie.manual.entry.lithography7=§lReading the interface§r<br>Five tanks are on the left. Hover over one to see what it holds and how much. An empty container in the slot below a tank drains it.<br><br>The lines at the bottom left are: the current step, the §lstatus§r (blue), the alignment rate and the light source.<br>When the machine stands still, read the blue line first. It says what is missing.
ie.manual.entry.lithography8=§lMaking a chip§r<br>With everything supplied, the machine works by itself.<br>Each layer takes five steps: oxidation, coating, align and expose, development, etching. The mask sets the number of layers.<br>Last come doping, metallization, resist strip, dicing and packaging.<br><br>The wafer is only used up when the chip is done. Take it out halfway and that chip is lost.
ie.manual.entry.lithography9=§lDevelopment§r<br>Negative resist is developed with solvent.<br>Positive resist takes water and alkali: water through the developer port, alkali in the two Alkali slots.<br><br>§lHow fine it can go§r<br>Light source and photoresist each have a finest line; the coarser one counts. If it is coarser than the mask's process node, the chip fails.<br>A scrapped chip states why in its tooltip.
ie.manual.entry.lithography10=§lAlignment§r<br>Before exposure, wafer and mask have to be lined up. This is done by hand.<br>Click Microscope in the interface to see a left and a right field. The dark cross is on the wafer, the pale frame is on the mask.<br>Turn the knobs until §lboth crosses sit in the middle of their frames§r, then click Confirm.
ie.manual.entry.lithography11=§lKnobs§r<br>X and Y shift both fields together.<br>θ rotates: the fields move opposite ways.<br>Low mag shows more, high mag more detail. Coarse steps are large, fine ones small.<br>Backlash: on reversing, the first bit does nothing.<br>One person turns them at a time.<br>§lKeyboard§r<br>Arrows shift, Q/E rotate, Shift for fine, Tab for mag, Enter confirms.
ie.manual.entry.lithography12=§lAlignment rate§r<br>The rate tells how well things line up. Below 50%% counts as not aligned, and the machine waits at the exposure step.<br>After every chip the stage drifts a little and the rate drops. Look in now and then, and realign when it has dropped too far.<br>After touching a knob, click Confirm again.
ie.manual.entry.lithography13=§lSwapping the light source§r<br>The status says when a light source is broken or empty. To swap it, take the machine apart: break any block and it turns back into its building blocks.<br>Items drop out, but §lfluids in the tanks are lost§r, so drain them first.<br>The light source stays in place and keeps its contents.<br>After forming the machine again, align again.
ie.manual.entry.lithography14=§lExcimer Laser Light Source§r<br>Masks with a finer process node are beyond the mercury lamp and ordinary resist. They need this light source, and a finer photoresist too.<br>It is crafted from an ArF Laser Cavity and a Floodlight and takes the place of the Mercury Light Source.<br>Exposure draws a lot of power.
ie.manual.entry.lithography15=§lArF Laser Cavity§r<br>The cavity is built with the Light Source blueprint. It comes out empty and you fill in the gases yourself.<br>How much of each goes in decides how strong the beam is. The tooltip shows the expected output.<br>The output drops with use; take the cavity out and top it up.
ie.manual.entry.lithography16=§lLaser Alignment§r<br>Lets the machine do the fine alignment and follow the drift after each chip.<br>It needs two things, in the two slots right of the chip outputs: a Personal Computer Motherboard holding the laser alignment control program, and a filled He-Ne Laser Tube.
ie.manual.entry.lithography17=§lUsing it§r<br>First align roughly with the knobs. Once the error is small enough, the Laser align button can be clicked. Click it and wait for the lock.<br>When the button cannot be clicked, the blue line below the microscope says why.<br>Touching a knob, taking the machine apart or drifting too far releases the laser. Then start over.
